    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear emphasis on high-upside leadoff hitters and speed-centric profiles. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98) is our anchor play; facing a middling arm in Brandon Pfaadt, the combination of a projected leadoff spot, significant platoon advantage, and the heavy weighting assigned to his elite sprint speed makes him the clear priority. Close behind him, **Junior Caminero** (74.78) and **Yordan Alvarez** (73.61) provide the necessary run-production ceiling. **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in core piece despite facing a competent Will Warren, largely because his 0.458 xwOBA effectively neutralizes the pitcher's efficiency. Meanwhile, **Lars Nootbaar** (62.07) earns a spot start over deeper options due to a favorable platoon split against Clay Holmes, bolstering our overall stolen base potential for the day.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Carson Benge** (36.49), who sat on the bubble but was ultimately excluded due to the daunting presence of Kyle Harrison on the mound. The combination of a left-on-left disadvantage and the optimizer's aggressive stance against pitchers with a sub-3.20 xERA caused **Carson Benge** to fall behind the scoring floor of our starting utility options. We are also proceeding with caution regarding **Dillon Dingler** (48.67) and **Colt Keith** (43.19); both are currently projected against Ian Seymour, an elite arm with a 3.21 xERA. While the \"slow delivery\" factor\u2014which aids our stolen base pursuit\u2014provides a marginal bump to their efficiency, they are operating at the lower threshold of our starting requirements and remain risks if the lineup construction shifts away from the top of the order. As of now, we are monitoring the status of **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** and **Caleb Durbin** closely, as the lack of official confirmed lineups adds a variance layer to our bottom-order production.",
